{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"Ozen","id":"321508500138360843","is_bot":0,"username":"ozenannwn"},"author_id":"321508500138360843","channel_id":"636012145204527127","content":"Can we get a steven universe star emoji? <@&736064286417223732>","created_at":"2020-08-19T18:09:25.044000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"745706000404709446","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"Ozen","id":"321508500138360843","is_bot":0,"username":"ozenannwn"},"author_id":"321508500138360843","channel_id":"636012145204527127","content":"Full honesty, one of the reasons I love roleplaying is that it offers some safety to step into other shoes and test socialization in ways that isn't safe in real life. Being autistic I can say rpgs has done more for figuring out social cues than any other single thing hands down. (even if I still have issues with that sometimes.) I really value that aspect of the format and now have some concerns if it isn't as safe a format for this type of exploration as I assumed. (I am completely ok with having NPCs or even PCs my Character doesn't like as long as it doesn't bleed out onto the players and DMs because I think conflict can be healthy for  story - so how do we keep it the healthy kind of conflict?)","created_at":"2020-08-19T18:13:07.500000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"745706933453062196","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
